{"term":"premise","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:bbb370bfc6aac352f3","definition":"A proposition antecedently supposed or proved; something previously stated or assumed as the basis of further argument; a condition; a supposition.","example":"The premises observed,\nThy will by my performance shall be served.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["counterpremise","off-premise","on-premise","premise indicator","major premise","minor premise"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/premise#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:53056a8303bd876803","definition":"Any of the first propositions of a syllogism, from which the conclusion is deduced.","example":"While the premises stand firm, 'tis impossible to shake the conclusion.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["counterpremise","off-premise","on-premise","premise indicator","major premise","minor premise"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/premise#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:7a484ddf8124fd371b","definition":"Matters previously stated or set forth; especially, that part in the beginning of a deed, the office of which is to express the grantor and grantee, and the land or thing granted or conveyed, and all that precedes the habendum; the thing demised or granted.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"plural-normally","register":"plural-normally","wordFamily":["counterpremise","off-premise","on-premise","premise indicator","major premise","minor premise"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/premise#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"i